WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.436
Plan how keys change before writing the
first encrypted record.

2
00:00:04.436 --> 00:00:07.623
A key is created, activated for specific
uses,

3
00:00:07.623 --> 00:00:11.278
rotated or retired, and eventually
destroyed according to policy.

4
00:00:11.278 --> 00:00:14.840
Retirement can stop new encryption while
allowing historical decryption.

5
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.089
Revocation or disabling access has service

6
00:00:17.089 --> 00:00:19.901
specific semantics and may stop required
operations.

7
00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:22.900
These states need explicit permissions
rather than one

8
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.462
ambiguous flag that means different things
to different callers.

9
00:00:26.462 --> 00:00:29.836
Keep a versioned encrypted representation
that can identify an

10
00:00:29.836 --> 00:00:33.397
approved format and the key needed for
historical data.

11
00:00:33.397 --> 00:00:35.272
Treat stored versions as unverified

12
00:00:35.272 --> 00:00:37.334
routing hints until authentication
succeeds.

13
00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:39.677
Resolve them through a controlled
registry,

14
00:00:39.677 --> 00:00:41.739
with purpose and environment restrictions.

15
00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:44.738
A key identifier must not become an
arbitrary

16
00:00:44.738 --> 00:00:47.737
path, network address, or unrestricted
secret lookup.

17
00:00:47.737 --> 00:00:49.987
The registry also determines which
historical

18
00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:52.798
uses remain permitted and which must fail.

19
00:00:52.798 --> 00:00:55.798
New write rotation selects a new key for

20
00:00:55.798 --> 00:00:59.359
future records while retaining authorized
access to older keys.

21
00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.796
It does not update existing ciphertext.

22
00:01:01.796 --> 00:01:04.514
Re-encryption migrates old records under
new material,

23
00:01:04.514 --> 00:01:06.857
preferably with bounded, resumable work
and

24
00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:09.669
conditional writes that avoid overwriting
concurrent changes.

25
00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.918
Envelope rewrapping changes protection
around data

26
00:01:11.918 --> 00:01:14.355
keys where the format permits it.

27
00:01:14.355 --> 00:01:18.198
Track which operation a migration actually
performs, verify that records

28
00:01:18.198 --> 00:01:22.603
remain readable, and preserve recovery
paths until the transition is complete.

29
00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:25.790
Emergency compromise response is different
from scheduled rotation.

30
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.789
Stopping new use does not erase copied
keys

31
00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:31.975
or undo exposure of historical data and
signatures.

32
00:01:31.975 --> 00:01:34.225
Assess affected material and decide what

33
00:01:34.225 --> 00:01:37.224
migration, revocation, or broader response
is necessary.

34
00:01:37.224 --> 00:01:39.473
Re-encryption cannot retract an older
ciphertext

35
00:01:39.473 --> 00:01:41.535
and key already obtained together.

36
00:01:41.535 --> 00:01:44.253
Before destroying a key, account for
required

37
00:01:44.253 --> 00:01:47.440
records, backups, replicas, exports, and
recovery procedures.

38
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.439
Deleting one key entry does not prove all

39
00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:53.532
copies are gone, and destroying the last
usable

40
00:01:53.532 --> 00:01:56.719
decryption key can make retained
ciphertext permanently unreadable.

41
00:01:56.719 --> 00:01:58.968
Lifecycle design must address both
confidentiality

42
00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:01.780
and the ability to recover authorized
data.

